I'm joined today by George Leakey who  recently retired from Swarthmore College  where he was Eugene and Lange visiting  professor for issues in social change  and manage the global nonviolent action  database research project Viking  economics is his ninth book and it's so  great to talk about it George let's  start sort of generally when we talk  about Viking economics or Nordic  economics Scandinavian economics talked  about it within different terms hugely  stigmatized in the United States by a  certain section of the sort of political  spectrum free stuff that people don't  deserve very high taxes and a bad  business environment right I mean is  there any truth to those stereotypes  not really for example Norway has more  startups per capita than the United  States does it's actually a very  thriving economic environment and they  do support entrepreneurs very strongly  they just don't support them at the  expense of the common good so that the  the genius of the what economists call  the Nordic model is that it keeps  looking out for the well-being of the  whole at the same time as it supports  creativity and innovation and the  economic realm there's this idea that  you know we could have a debate over  what is the sort of right or fair level  of Taxation and that sort of a more uh  abstract question but there's this idea  that objectively taxes are just  outrageously high in many of these  countries but I think what's often not  considered in that is if you are paying  for example health insurance premiums in  the US and you're comparing the tax rate  to a country that has a national health  care system shouldn't you also be  considering your health insurance  premiums into in thinking about sort of  your total tax rate if comparing to a  country that has a national health care  system I mean I am I wrong about that or  would that be how no they didn't a price  to apples comparison that's right that's  you're completely right in fact when you  ask entrepreneurs who have achieved you  know some  well when you ask them well how do you  feel about these very high taxes that  you pay they said yes but we don't pay  for health insurance we don't need to  send our we don't need to pay for higher  education for our kids we can send our  kids through medical school law school  whatever and we're not actually sending  them through because they're going  through thanks to a an economic system  that makes those in a sense free of  course nothing is really free it's all  paid by taxation on the other hand the  benefits are enormous and many Americans  for example living over there would  actually save money because especially  if they're paying for their own medical  care which can be of course enormous it  can even bankrupt people and also and  nobody's bankrupted by medical care over  there because it's all taken care of  and also map matters like higher  education which again can be something a  lot of young people are now saddled with  enormous debts but that's that's unheard  of in that in that in those countries  let's assume for a second that we could  convince some of these I don't know if  we want to call them supply siders or  call them what you will those who are  very negative about the Nordic economic  system let's assume we could convince  someone that it works well they're right  very often we run up against this  talking point that listen the US can't  do what smaller European countries can  do in terms of health care or social  services etcetera because those  countries are very small and the u.s. is  very big many more people much more  infrastructure and what works there just  could not be applied in the United  States but talk about that a little bit  is there anything to that well the same  thing was said about social security it  would never work for the United States  to have a social security system that's  something Europeans can do but we cannot  and obviously we're well established in  the Social Security system  another example more recent example is  Medicare because again there was a lot  of opposition among supply siders to  having Medicare not only is Medicare  counted on by by tens of millions of  Americans and all over the country but  also  but studies are done about how satisfied  people are with Medicare it turns out  people are much more satisfied with  Medicare than they are with private  individual health insurance and so those  are two systems that are taken for  granted in the Nordic countries they  were imported to the United States and  they get a huge support here in the  United States there's another of sort of  common line from the detractors of many  of the systems that you wrote about in  Viking economics which is what's  different really is not a matter of  scale when you compare the American  economy to Nordic economies but what's  different is that many of those  countries are relatively homogenous and  have less diversity and this is a very  popular talking point for those who are  against uh multiculturalism and  diversity etcetera they say hey the US  could do what they're doing there but we  can't in practice because we just let  anybody into this country and it's too  diverse here and and we have all of the  problems that come with diversity as  they see it talk a little bit about that  with regard to some of these economies  well it was an important research  question for me as well because I didn't  look into those countries not  skeptically you know I was very curious  about how do they handle questions like  diversity and and for that matter  questions of scale and now could that be  applied to our country and on my way  into one of the research outfits that  gave me generously a tremendous lot of  time for interviewing I saw a photograph  on the wall that showed a delegation of  Chinese who had been sent from Beijing  and I said oh why why was this Chinese  delegation here and they said well these  were economists and political scientists  and policymakers who were sent from  Beijing to explore and learn from the  Nohr wage and system and I said how  could that possibly be for exactly the  reasons you just stated and they said  that they asked that same question of  the Chinese they were very surprised the  Chinese were they're tiny you know  Norway is only five million people on  the end and of course China is not only  huge compared with Norway China is huge  compared with the United States and  China is also highly diverse more much  more diverse than the United States is  and so what they the Tiny's delegation  said is our government sent us here to  learn what we can because some  particular policies and structures are  influenced by diversity are influenced  by scale and others are not some things  scale up really well and the Social  Security would be an example of that and  some some things can are very very  doable no matter how much a diversity  there is and again social security would  be an example of that in our own country  because in our own experience Medicare  and Social Security don't aren't  affected by uh you know by the the first  language people happen to speak there  are probably many people who are sort of  generally familiar in my audience with  uh the with Scandinavia and Nordic  economic models but they may sort of see  them as as an amorphous non  distinguished group in your research and  this may be as much of a cultural  question as it is economic what were  some of the most interesting  distinguishing factors between the  countries you studied that might be  interesting to some in our audience one  distinguishing factor is that the  Norwegians are far more distrustful of  corporations of large corporations than  the other countries they're so  distrustful that the Norwegian  government bias controlling interests of  all the large corporations and so when  the Board of Directors meets a certain  number of those seeds you know the  majority actually the controlling  interests are held by the government  itself and that's in order to make sure  that those corporations don't do things  that are against the public interest  that that's a more I would say the know  reasons are more distrustful of  corporate decision making than the other  Nordic countries very very interesting  uh last thing I want to touch on when it  comes to looking at issues like for  example climate change or more uh what  we might consider social issues  you talked a little bit about how the  economic system of the country sort of  influences the views on some of these  other issues that that certainly are  related to economics but maybe less so  in some ways yes well I do have a couple  of chapters in my book Viking economics  on the the things that I say are both  challenges for our country for America  and also challenges for them one  challenge is racism  yes racism does show up in those  countries and as it does so so  prominently in ours and the the what the  reason why they are more optimistic  actually than we are about handling  racism is because they have the Nordic  economic model on their side so for  example people with talent to go to  university are not handicapped by their  financial situation they may be very  recent immigrants they may come from you  know a very impoverished countries that  have their previous life and then  suddenly there they are in Norway or  Sweden or Denmark and they're able to go  all you know all the way as far as their  talent will take them and so that means  that the even though the family they  came with maybe it could remain  desperate poor for a while until they  get their feet under under them the  members of their family that are able to  take advantage of the tremendous  training and educational opportunities  there can just go right for it they're  not frozen out for a few generations  while people slowly try to get as often  is the case here try to get into the  system for upward mobility so those  countries have what much more upward  mobility than the United States does it  used to be the reverse it used to be  they had person you know if you were  born into working class that's where you  stayed but over there  there's my now in mobility which me  because the economy in turn is benefited  by being rewarded and people being able  to move forward on the things that  really matter to them we have been  speaking about the book Viking economics  which is George Lake he's nine  book thanks so much for talking to us  about this very very interesting you're  welcome very happy to be here
 

